## 2.4 Handover Mechanism: Terrestrial-to-Space Continuity

A major engineering barrier in ubiquitous connectivity is managing **handovers**—ensuring an active video or voice call does not abruptly drop when transitioning between cell towers and space satellites.

```
                     HANDOVER TIMELINE & DECISION TREE

  Terrestrial Signal (5G)
  =======================> [ Degradation Zone ] ────► [ Signal Lost ]
                                    │
  Handover Trigger                  ▼
  (PDU Session Transfer)  [ Start Satellite Search ]
                                    │
                                    ▼
  Space Link (NTN)        [ Pre-Sync Ephemeris ] ───► [ Space Session Active ]
  ================================───────────────────────────────────────────►

```

The gateway manages this using a **Predictive Vertical Handover Protocol**:

* **Soft Degradation Thresholds:** The gateway continuously monitors terrestrial signal quality. When the primary signal degrades past a defined RSSI limit, the gateway wakes the NTN modem from standby mode to begin satellite tracking *before* the ground connection completely drops.
* **Ephemeris-Driven Satellite Synchronization:** Rather than scanning broad radio frequencies (which drains battery), the gateway reads SIB19 system information blocks and local GPS data to pinpoint the exact time and frequency coordinates of incoming satellites.
* **PDU Session Continuity:** Utilizing 5G Core Multi-Access PDU (MA-PDU) session protocols, the gateway maintains the same user IP address across both interfaces, enabling continuous data sessions without connection resets.

